<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/04ZP">Example <span data-tag="04ZP">1.1.2.8</span> <span class="named">(Degenerate $2$-Simplices)</span>.</a> Let $S_{\bullet }$ be a simplicial set and let $\sigma $ be a $2$-simplex of $S_{\bullet }$. We say that $\sigma $ is <em>left-degenerate</em> if it has the form $s^{1}_{0}(e)$, for some edge $e: x \rightarrow y$ of $S_{\bullet }$. In this case, the faces of $\sigma $ are depicted in the diagram </p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  \xymatrix { &amp;  x \ar [dr]^{e} &amp;  \\ x \ar [ur]^{\operatorname{id}_{x}} \ar [rr]^{e} &amp;  &amp;  y. } \]
</div>
<p> We will say that $\sigma $ is <em>right-degenerate</em> if it has the form $s^{1}_{1}(e)$, for some edge $e: x \rightarrow y$ of $S_{\bullet }$; in this case, the faces of $\sigma $ are depicted in the diagram </p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  \xymatrix { &amp;  y \ar [dr]^{\operatorname{id}_ y} &amp;  \\ x \ar [ur]^{e} \ar [rr]^{e} &amp;  &amp;  y. } \]
</div>
<p> Note that $\sigma $ is degenerate if and only if it is either left-degenerate or right-degenerate. </p>
</article>
